WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.640
Safe binary parsing in C begins with a simple rule: treat binary input as untrusted until the relevant format rules have been checked.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:13.700
A byte does not become a length, offset, count, flag, record, or command just because it is present.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.640
The parser gives it meaning only after validating that it belongs to the expected contract.

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:21.900
Explicit decoding is one of the strongest habits you can build.

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:25.560
Decode exact integer widths and byte order deliberately.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:28.740
Avoid blind casts from raw bytes to C structs.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:33.040
Keep file or protocol layout separate from internal trusted objects.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:41.500
That separation improves portability, makes validation easier to review, and reduces accidental dependence on compiler or architecture details.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:52.760
The most important values to validate are the ones that influence memory and control flow: lengths, offsets, counts, numeric ranges, section boundaries, and cross-field relationships.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:57.780
Check arithmetic before allocation, indexing, copying, or pointer movement.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:04.800
A valid start offset is not enough; the full range must be valid, and the calculation that finds the end must not wrap.

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:08.380
Parser failure should be ordinary, safe, and predictable.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:14.040
Invalid input should not produce partial trusted state, excessive resource use, or confusing cleanup.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:21.540
A strong parser fails closed, releases what it owns, and reports enough context for debugging without depending on unsafe assumptions.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.240
Finally, use layers of support.

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:33.180
Unit tests, corpus tests, fuzzing, sanitizers, static analysis, and disciplined review all catch different kinds of mistakes.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:38.200
None of them replaces careful design, but together they make parser behavior more reliable.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:45.380
Binary-safe C is not one trick; it is a habit of making every assumption visible, validated, and testable.
